['Here's a simple C code implementation of atoi() function using command line arguments:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int my_atoi(const char *str) {
    int num = 0;
    int sign = 1;

    if (*str == '-') {
        sign = -1;
        str++;
    } else if (*str == '+') {
        str++;
    }

    while (*str != '\0' && *str >= '0' && *str <= '9') {
        num = num * 10 + (*str - '0');
        str++;
    }

    return sign * num;
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc > 1) {
        int num = my_atoi(argv[1]);
        printf("atoi: %s -> %d\n", argv[1], num);
    } else {
        printf("Usage: %s <integer>\n", argv[0]);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, the `my_atoi()` function takes a string as input and converts it to an integer. The main() function demonstrates how to use the `my_atoi()` function by converting the first command line argument (after the program name) to an integer.

The `main()` function checks if there are more than one command line arguments using `argc`. If there is, it calls `my_atoi()` with the second command line argument and prints out the result.']

